<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caradog ap Gruffydd</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1081">1081</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caradog ap Gruffydd was a Welsh ruler of Gwent and Morgannwg in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in the later 11th century, rising after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gruffydd-ap-llywelyn">Gruffudd ap Llywelyn</a>. Seeking to dominate south Wales, he was defeated and killed by Gruffudd ap Cynan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-tewdwr">Rhys ap Tewdwr</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mynydd-carn">Battle of Mynydd Carn</a> in 1081.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Caradog belonged to a dynasty whose strongholds were the kingdoms of Glywysing and Gwent. His grandfather Rhydderch ab Iestyn, King of Glywysing, had held the throne of Deheubarth from 1023 until his death in 1033. His father, Gruffudd ap Rhydderch, received the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caerleon">Caerleon</a> in 1031, inherited Glywysing and became King of Deheubarth in 1045. The family descended from Owain, son of Morgan Hen, the last ruler of a unified Kingdom of Morgannwg.</p><p>Gruffudd ap Rhydderch was said to have resisted raids by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> and attacks by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gruffydd-ap-llywelyn">Gruffudd ap Llywelyn</a>, but in 1055 Gruffudd ap Llywelyn killed him in battle and took Deheubarth. After a further victory near Glasbury in 1056, Gruffudd ap Llywelyn was recognised as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> by 1057, and Caradog received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caerleon">Caerleon</a>. From the family's base in Gwent he appears to have added his inheritance in Morgannwg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gruffydd-ap-llywelyn">Gruffudd ap Llywelyn</a> in 1063, the old lineages of the south were restored. Caradog became ruler of Glywysing at about the time Maredudd ab Owain ab Edwin, the male-line heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a>, took Deheubarth. In 1065 Caradog destroyed the hunting lodge Harold was building at Portskewett and ravaged the district. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> sacked south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and parts of Gwent, and Maredudd, who chose not to resist them, was rewarded with lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1070.</p><p>Caradog then set out to add Deheubarth to his realm, as his father and grandfather had done. In 1072 he defeated and killed Maredudd in a battle by the Rhymney River, and in 1074 he took control of what was left of war-ravaged Gwent from his kinsman Cadwgan ap Meurig. In 1078 he killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ab-owain">Rhys ab Owain</a>, Maredudd's successor, and by 1081 he had forced the next prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-tewdwr">Rhys ap Tewdwr</a>, to flee to St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s Cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhys-ap-tewdwr">Rhys ap Tewdwr</a> found an ally in Gruffudd ap Cynan, who had come from Ireland to seize Gwynedd from Trahaearn ap Caradog; the two met at St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s and made their alliance with the blessing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of St Davids. Caradog answered by allying with Trahaearn. The armies met at Mynydd Carn, about a day's march north of St Davids, and Caradog and Trahaearn were both killed. His son Owain ap Caradog kept Gwynllwg and founded the line of the Lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caerleon">Caerleon</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iestyn-ap-gwrgant">Iestyn ap Gwrgant</a> became King of Morgannwg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caradog ap Gruffudd” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its last king, <b>Caradog ap Gruffudd</b>, was killed at the battle of Mynydd Carn in 1081, and Gwent became Norman marcher lordships, later Monmouthshire.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gwent">Kingdom of Gwent</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in 974 Gwent and Glywysing separated again at times, and the kingdom was later ruled by Gruffydd ap Llywelyn and then <b>Caradog ap Gruffydd</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-morgannwg">Kingdom of Morgannwg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mynydd-carn">Battle of Mynydd Carn</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.76,-4.76&amp;year=1071" aria-label="Battle of Mynydd Carn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Caradog ap Gruffydd"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4748 5693h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4748 5693h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.76,-4.76&amp;year=1071">What was here in 1071? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
